   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the alcoholic beverage control law, in relation to exempting\ncertain parcels of land from licensing restrictions prohibiting manufacturers,\nwholesalers and retailers of alcoholic beverages from sharing an interest in a\nlicensed premises",
    "basePrintNo": "S9092",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S9092-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §101, ABC L",
    "lawSection": "Alcoholic Beverage Control Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S9092\n\nSPONSOR: BROUK\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the alcoholic beverage control law, in relation to\nexempting certain parcels of land from licensing restrictions prohibit-\ning manufacturers, wholesalers and retailers of alcoholic beverages from\nsharing an interest in a licensed premises\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo add parcels of land, by metes and bounds description, to the list of\npremises which are exempt from the provisions of law which generally\nrestrict manufacturers/wholesalers/importers and retailers from sharing\nan interest in a liquor license.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 inserts the retailer metes and bounds into section 101 of ABC\nlaw.\n\nSection 2 inserts the retailer metes and bounds into section 101 of ABC\nlaw.\n\nSection 3 insets language of metes and bounds for the wholesaler or\nManufacture into section 106 of ABC law.\n\nSection 4 is the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThrough what are known as the \"tied-house\" laws -- New York State\nrestricts holders of alcohol manufacturing licenses from having an\ninterest in a retail license. The law is occasionally amended to exempt\ncertain properties from this restriction as this bill would do, when\nthere is not a significant risk of undue market influence.\n\nThese provisions have been amended on occasion by exempting certain\nproperties to support economic development goals. Exemptions, such as\nthe one provided by this bill, are narrowly tailored to prevent undue\nmarket influence by identifying select parcels using metes and bounds\ndescriptions.\n\nThis bill would allow a manufacturers licensee to also hold an interest\nin a retail licensee and vice versa. The licensees are in Rochester,\nLake Placid and Shelby, further described by the metes and bounds\ndescriptions included in the bill. In this instance, the manufacture\nlicensee is making an ingredient not a final product for retail. As\nsuch, the risk of undue influence between the manufacture licensee and\nthe retail licensee is limited, if any. The license application. would\nstill be subject to the normal approval process.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
